+# TypeScript pilot
+
+This pilot connects TypeScript exercises to Mathua's existing generator interface.
+It uses the existing engine, HTTP routes, SQLite store, prerequisite rules, mastery model and report workflow.
+It serves Mathua's existing study interface beside the API.
+The three pilot concepts have short explanations in Portuguese.
+
+The TypeScript content and grader are a separate adaptation.
+They are not Math Academy content or an official Mathua curriculum.
+
+## Scope
+
+| Concept ID | Observable task | Prerequisites in this pilot |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `ts.value.numeric_literal` | Write a numeric literal for a stated value | None |
+| `ts.type.number` | Name the numeric type in a type annotation | None |
+| `ts.binding.const_number` | Declare a constant with the requested name and numeric literal | Both concepts above |
+
+The prerequisite edges are a teaching choice.
+They do not define a mandatory sequence for all TypeScript learning.
+The native mastery thresholds remain in `internal/mastery/evidence.go`.
+The pilot does not validate their educational calibration.
+
+Each concept has one exercise family.
+The generator varies the value and, where applicable, the identifier.
+These parameter changes do not create a new exercise family.
+Generation uses a local draw counter because the registry reuses one context within a batch.
+A seed alone does not reproduce a question without the counter state.
+Reports preserve the question, expected answer and learner answer for replay.
+
+## Install and test
+
+Use the Go version declared in the repository's `go.mod`.
+Use a Node version that satisfies `web/next-app/package.json` and install npm.
+Run these commands from the repository root:
+
+```sh
+npm ci --prefix cmd/typescript-pilot --ignore-scripts
+npm ci --prefix web/next-app
+NEXT_PUBLIC_SAME_ORIGIN_API=1 NEXT_TELEMETRY_DISABLED=1 npm run build --prefix web/next-app
+go test ./cmd/typescript-pilot -count=1 -v
+go build -o pilot-bin ./cmd/typescript-pilot
+./pilot-bin
+```
+
+Open `http://127.0.0.1:8081` in a browser.
+The root redirects to the first concept in the existing `/learn` page.
+The exported frontend uses the API on the same origin.
+Select `Next` to start and use `Practice again` to continue a concept.
+The native section completion card and the engine's mastery state have different criteria.
+Use the next-concept link to continue after the engine marks a concept as mastered.
+The controls keep the original interface language.
+
+The command prints its local study and API addresses.
+Use `-addr` to set the listen address.
+Use `-db` to set the SQLite database path.
+Keep the adjacent `.jwt` file with the database to preserve login tokens across process restarts.
+Use `-node` or `-grader` to set non-default executable and script paths.
+The lesson directory sits beside `grade.cjs`.
+Use `-web` to set the static export directory.
+Run the binary from the repository root with the default paths.
+Without a frontend build, the API still starts and the command prints the build instruction.
+
+## Existing routes used by the test
+
+| Operation | Native route |
+| --- | --- |
+| Create learner identity | `POST /api/auth/guest` |
+| Request exercises | `GET /api/lessons/{concept_id}/practice` |
+| Submit an answer | `POST /api/study/answer` |
+| Report a question | `POST /api/reports` |
+| Read report resolutions | `GET /api/reports/mine` |
+| Replay a reported answer | `POST /api/admin/reports/{id}/grade` |
+| Resolve a report | `PATCH /api/admin/reports/{id}` |
+
+Send the returned bearer token on learner requests.
+Send the served question text and a unique `submission_id` with each logical answer.
+Reuse that ID when retrying the same submission.
+Request the next variant with `count=1` and `exclude` set to the previous question text.
+The test supplies actual JSON requests and checks the persisted state.
+Administrative routes use the existing role checks.
+The test grants an operator role only inside its temporary database.
+
+## Grading contract
+
+The constant prompt states that a `number` annotation is optional.
+Both answers below satisfy that task:
+
+```ts
+const amount: number = 15000;
+const amount = 15000;
+```
+
+Each submission contains only one of these declarations.
+The checker rejects `let`, an incorrect name, an incorrect value, a string initializer or extra statements.
+It accepts numeric separators, whitespace and ordinary comments.
+It checks TypeScript syntax and types without executing or emitting learner code.
+Compiler file reads are restricted to the virtual answer and TypeScript's standard library declarations.
+Compiler failure returns an unavailable result instead of a learner error.
+The added `ungraded` field transmits that distinction on an unavailable result.
+
+### Optional `ungraded` field
+
+Treat `ungraded === true` as "the grader failed, no answer was evaluated".
+For a successful HTTP response, an absent field or `false` means the answer was evaluated.
+Use `correct` to distinguish a correct answer from an incorrect one.
+Handle HTTP failures before interpreting these fields.
+
+With `submission_id`, the engine stores normal answer JSON in `ResponseJSON`.
+That JSON omits `ungraded` and the server returns it before the fallback response map.
+Retries return the same saved body.
+The existing `LearnStepper` already checks `if (res.ungraded)`.
+This pilot preserves that optional-field contract and documents it instead of changing stored JSON.
+
+## Feedback and limits
+
+A report stores the concrete exercise and answer.
+The study page passes the expected answer and explanation to its existing report button after grading.
+A reviewer can replay it with the same grader, record a resolution and return that resolution to the learner.
+Reporting and replay do not write learning evidence.
+The native `repair.Run` operation can correct historical false negatives after a grader change.
+The test checks its preview and apply paths with a simulated historical error.
+That repair does not recalculate XP.
+
+Feedback does not automatically generate new exercise families or tune selection in this pilot.
+A reviewer still decides how to change a prompt or grader.
+The prerequisite test covers practice and study submission without diagnostic placement.
+The original diagnostic placement policy remains available in Mathua.
+The API test restarts the server and reopens SQLite within the test process.
+The companion `anki-typescript` repository supplies `process-smoke.py` for real process restarts
+and `browser-smoke.mjs` for the existing interface against the real API.
+These checks do not measure a deployed installation or long-term retention.
diff --git a/cmd/typescript-pilot/grade.cjs b/cmd/typescript-pilot/grade.cjs
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..2faecb0
--- /dev/null
+++ b/cmd/typescript-pilot/grade.cjs
@@ -0,0 +1,88 @@
+'use strict';
+
+// This is a narrow static grader. Learner code is never evaluated or emitted.
+const ts = require('typescript');
+const fs = require('node:fs');
+const path = require('node:path');
+
+const result = (correct, feedback) => ({correct, score: correct ? 1 : 0, feedback});
+const parse = source => ts.createSourceFile('answer.ts', source, ts.ScriptTarget.ES2022, true);
+
+function declaration(sf) {
+  const statement = sf.statements.length === 1 && sf.statements[0];
+  if (!statement || !ts.isVariableStatement(statement) || statement.modifiers?.length) return null;
+  const list = statement.declarationList;
+  if (list.declarations.length !== 1) return null;
+  return {list, item: list.declarations[0]};
+}
+
+function typeErrors(source) {
+  const filename = path.resolve(__dirname, '__answer__.ts');
+  const options = {strict: true, noEmit: true, types: [], skipLibCheck: true, target: ts.ScriptTarget.ES2022};
+  const host = ts.createCompilerHost(options);
+  const library = path.dirname(ts.getDefaultLibFilePath(options));
+  const read = host.readFile.bind(host);
+  // Only compiler library files are accessible. Triple-slash paths in an answer
+  // cannot make the checker read other project or host files.
+  const allowed = file => path.dirname(path.resolve(file)) === library && /^lib(?:\..+)?\.d\.ts$/.test(path.basename(file));
+  host.readFile = file => path.resolve(file) === filename ? source : allowed(file) ? read(file) : undefined;
+  host.fileExists = file => path.resolve(file) === filename || (allowed(file) && ts.sys.fileExists(file));
+  host.getSourceFile = (file, languageVersion) => {
+    const content = host.readFile(file);
+    return content === undefined ? undefined : ts.createSourceFile(file, content, languageVersion, true);
+  };
+  const program = ts.createProgram([filename], options, host);
+  return ts.getPreEmitDiagnostics(program).map(d => ts.flattenDiagnosticMessageText(d.messageText, '\n'));
+}
+
+function grade({kind, expected, answer}) {
+  if (typeof expected !== 'string' || typeof answer !== 'string') throw new Error('Invalid grader input');
+  if (!answer.trim()) return result(false, 'Digite a resposta solicitada.');
+  if (answer.length > 4096) return result(false, 'A resposta excede o tamanho deste exercício.');
+  if (kind === 'type') {
+    if (expected !== 'number') throw new Error('Invalid reference type');
+    return result(answer.trim() === 'number', answer.trim() === 'number'
+      ? 'Correto. number nomeia o tipo numérico.'
+      : 'Use number para nomear o tipo numérico. Um literal como 15000 representa um valor, não o nome do tipo.');
+  }
+  const sf = parse(answer);
+  if (sf.parseDiagnostics.length) return result(false, 'A resposta contém um erro de sintaxe TypeScript.');
+  if (kind === 'literal') {
+    const stmt = sf.statements.length === 1 && sf.statements[0];
+    const expr = stmt && ts.isExpressionStatement(stmt) && stmt.expression;
+    if (!expr || !ts.isNumericLiteral(expr)) return result(false, 'Use somente um literal numérico, sem aspas e sem operações.');
+    if (Number(expr.text) !== Number(expected)) return result(false, 'O literal representa um valor diferente do solicitado.');
+    return result(true, 'Correto. O literal representa o valor solicitado.');
+  }
+  if (kind !== 'const') throw new Error('Unknown exercise kind');
+  const reference = declaration(parse(expected));
+  if (!reference || !ts.isIdentifier(reference.item.name) || !ts.isNumericLiteral(reference.item.initializer)) {
+    throw new Error('Invalid reference declaration');
+  }
+  const actual = declaration(sf);
+  if (!actual) return result(false, 'Escreva somente uma declaração de constante, com um único nome.');
+  if (!(actual.list.flags & ts.NodeFlags.Const)) return result(false, 'A tarefa pede const, que impede reatribuir esse nome após a inicialização. let permite reatribuição.');
+  if (!ts.isIdentifier(actual.item.name) || actual.item.name.text !== reference.item.name.text) {
+    return result(false, `Use o nome solicitado: ${reference.item.name.text}.`);
+  }
+  const init = actual.item.initializer;
+  if (!init || !ts.isNumericLiteral(init)) return result(false, 'Inicialize a constante com um literal numérico, sem aspas e sem operações.');
+  if (Number(init.text) !== Number(reference.item.initializer.text)) return result(false, 'O valor inicial difere do solicitado.');
+  if (actual.item.type && actual.item.type.kind !== ts.SyntaxKind.NumberKeyword) {
+    return result(false, 'Neste exercício, omita a anotação ou use number, como indicado no enunciado.');
+  }
+  const errors = typeErrors(answer);
+  if (errors.length) return result(false, `O compilador TypeScript rejeitou a declaração: ${errors[0]}`);
+  return result(true, 'Correto. A constante tem o nome e o valor solicitados. A anotação number é opcional neste exercício.');
+}
+
+if (require.main === module) {
+  try {
+    process.stdout.write(JSON.stringify(grade(JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(0, 'utf8')))));
+  } catch (error) {
+    process.stderr.write(error.message + '\n');
+    process.exitCode = 1;
+  }
+}
+
+module.exports = {grade};
